WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.450
Fun fact! Did you know that I started on YouTube in
2011, the same year Ernest Cline wrote Ready

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.450
Player One?

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:07.690
And look at us now!

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:11.440
We both have published books--nope, I guess
just him.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:17.441
But we both have millions of dollar in incensing
and residuals-noooo again that’s  him and

00:00:17.441 --> 00:00:18.441
not me.

00:00:18.441 --> 00:00:28.000
Ooh, but hey a blockbuster movie hitting theaters
noooo time soon again that's just Ernest Cline. Well shoot what have I been doing with all my time?

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.860
SCREW YOU Ernest, no one likes an overachiever.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.280
Hello Internet, and welcome to Film Theory,
where the one thing I DO have in common with

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.600
Ernest Cline is that we’re both riding the
nostalgia wave to get people to care about

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.880
us. Feels good, man.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:00:59.860
Feels good.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.659
As one of the hardest core nerds out there,
you’d think I’d be sweating through my

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:04.689
tube socks at the idea of Ready Player One.

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:09.380
Set in 2044, it’s a futuristic sci-fi movie
about a treasure hunt happening in the most

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:15.530
advanced massive multiplayer online game in
history, called OASIS, or "Ontologically Anthropocentric

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.579
Sensory Immersive Simulation."

00:01:17.579 --> 00:01:22.560
It’s got the good guy, Wade, fighting the
evil corporation to find the ultimate video

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.070
game easter egg that promises to give the
player a massive fortune and control over

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:28.310
the entire game.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:32.549
The premise is truly impressive, but there
are a few bones I gotta pick with this thinig.

00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:36.500
You see, in the movie the reason why most
people spend all their time in the OASIS is

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.010
that most of the population lives in IRL poverty,
including our hero, Wade.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:44.509
And hey, I’m all about the underdog coming
out of nothing and fighting the powers that

00:01:44.509 --> 00:01:48.159
be--it’s the story of every good YouTuber
I know--but the world of Ready Player One

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:53.869
gets so ridiculous sometimes that if you actually
look it them using real sci- instead of sci-fi,

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:57.200
our buddy Wade literally wouldn’t survive
the opening credits.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.509
And no, I’m not talking about some super
advanced technology thing where someone’s

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:04.350
getting zapped with fiber optic cables or
getting their memory wiped by their own haptic

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:05.350
suit.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.660
Let’s face it, by 2044, technology will
be so advanced that there’s really no way

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:13.069
to predict the state of video games or personal
technology--I can only sit here and salivate

00:02:13.069 --> 00:02:16.990
thinking about how great it’ll be and start
worshipping my Google overlords now.

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:21.950
No, I’m talking way more basic than that:
the biggest threat in this movie isn’t technology

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:27.400
of evil corporations or even really bad eye
strain--it’s the engineering of everyone’s

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.400
home.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:35.900
That’s right, in a movie about super futuristic
video games, I’m focused on the civil engineering.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:48.819
And isn’t that why you come here?

00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:57.540
Oh, and fair warning: After this episode,
you’ll never want to visit the Empire State

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.900
Building ever again.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.230
Wade lives in the “Stacks:” monstrous
scrap piles of low-income housing that are

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.800
basically upright piles of single-wide trailer
homes.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:15.170
According to the book they’re over 20 levels
high -- twice as tall as the smallest skyscrapers.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:19.860
Now in the movie we see the basic structure
of these things: single trailers stuck all

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:24.051
over the place on what the book describes
as “modular scaffolds” made so that they

00:03:24.051 --> 00:03:30.090
can just keep stacking these things as high
as they need to go.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.689
As more people move to the area, more trailers
get added to the top.

00:03:33.689 --> 00:03:38.069
But this creates a major support problem that
anyone with a little engineering know-how

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:39.260
can immediately see.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:43.030
It’s not something you really think about
all that often, but turns out there are some

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:47.079
pretty strong reasons why you don’t just
“add on” to skyscrapers after they’re

00:03:47.079 --> 00:03:52.530
finished, your trailer stack just becomes
a really deadly game of Jenga.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:57.569
Starting at the bottom, the Stacks have major
problems with the foundation.

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:01.599
The bigger (and heavier) any structure is,
the stronger the foundation you need.

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:06.500
The foundation for a 1-story house might have
a depth of only 1 meter, but skycrapers have

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:11.170
foundations that go down 50 meters or more,
depending on soil underneath it.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:15.700
To get to a solid base, you sometimes have
to go down hundreds of feet to avoid the building

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.570
sinking into soft spots later on.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:22.201
The Petronas Twin Tower in Kuala Lumpur has
the deepest foundation of any skyscraper in

00:04:22.201 --> 00:04:28.390
the world, ranging from 200 to 374 feet in
different places along its base because it’s

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:29.790
built on soft rock.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.290
Why does the building sink?

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:35.170
Because of something in civil engineering
called compression force, which is the force

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:38.470
exerted by the top layers of a building on
the bottom layers.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:43.160
Think about it: each layer of a building has
to hold up ALL the layers that get stacked

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:44.160
on top of it.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.160
That means that your bottom layers have to
be the strongest and most stable, since they’re

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.580
holding the most weight.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:54.030
Applying this to the Stacks in the movie,
already you can see the issue with the "modular"

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:55.030
approach.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:58.790
Real buildings calculate how much weight they’re
going to support in advance.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:03.330
A modular building like the Stacks isn’t
doing that.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:07.600
Every layer you add causes the scaffolds and
all the mobile homes on lower levels to compress

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.000
a little bit more, meaning that without a
foundation underneath, eventually these stacks

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.600
are going to turn into sinks.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:22.090
I mean you could over-build those bottom layers
on the CHANCE that you have to add more layers

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:28.270
to the top, but then you’re wasting a lot
of money and resources.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:34.920
Another alternative is that, yes, the Stacks
might not have foundations, but you don’t

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.030
have to build deep if you build wide.

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:40.440
Think like the pyramids, or, I should say,
the pyramids after they figured out that they

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.710
need to pay attention to compression force.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:45.780
You want to see what version 1.0 of the Pyramids
looked like?

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:49.630
Here’s the Bent Pyramid, for example.

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:52.160
This is a weird one, right?

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.940
I’ve been to Cairo and seen this thing,
which is just as bizarre looking in person.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.620
There are a few theories on why this pyramid
is this derpy outcast shape, and when you

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.540
discount the crazy theories like alien intervention,
you’re left with the exact same problem

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.610
we’re facing in Ready Player One.

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:12.120
In the case of the Bent Pyramid, the bottom
layers are made of limestone, which is a relatively

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.870
soft stone that expands and contracts with
moisture.

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:20.760
When the early Egyptians started building
the structure, the slope of the sides was

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:25.600
too steep, creating too much compressive force
on the foundations as the structure got higher,

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.800
causing the limestone to crush down.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:32.180
The builders changed angles mid-way up the
pyramid to lessen the pressure on the structure

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:35.470
to avoid the stones compressing and warping
over time.

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:40.170
So wide and flat IS an option, but it can
only happen in places where you’re not having

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:41.480
to account for weather.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:46.170
The only place you find wide, flat foundations
today is in warm-weather climates like California

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:52.200
and Arizona; in colder climates that hit freezing
temperatures every winter, the ground freezes

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:58.950
and cracks a shallow foundation made of something
like concrete.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:04.530
Ready Player One takes place in Columbus Ohio,
which gets 22 inches of snow per year and

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:06.990
makes this kind of foundation a serious no-no.

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:12.970
But if the whole my-trailer-stack-is-sinking-into-the-frozen-Ohio-ground
isn’t enough, we’ve actually got bigger

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:13.970
problems: the wind.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:19.450
In addition to the vertical force of gravity,
skyscrapers also have to deal with the horizontal

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:24.230
force of wind and that means so do our friends
hanging in the Stacks.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:27.790
Wind-dampening systems are actually incredibly
high-tech and require some of the most advanced

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:30.560
engineering in the world--not inexpensive
engineering, either, if you’re looking to

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.870
pay for your Stacks with fewer, well, stacks.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:38.240
For example, the Citicorp Center in New York
City uses what’s called a tuned mass-damper

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.940
to counteract the force of wind on the skyscraper.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:45.400
These types of systems electronically sense
shifting pressure in the building due to wind.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:50.370
The damper then uses hydraulic systems to
quickly push a 400-ton concrete slab weight

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:54.270
back and forth across the top of the building
to shift the structure’s center of gravity

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:57.400
from side to side, counteracting the force
of the wind.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.680
So yeah, if you ever visit one of those “world’s
tallest buildings,” just know that there’s

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.400
probably a sliding slab of concrete floating
over your head the size of a meteor.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:07.580
No pressure.

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:11.680
But even those high tech systems sometimes
aren’t enough, especially when you’re

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:15.180
dealing with the same issues that Ready Player
One is dealing with: namely, cost.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.720
Let’s look at the Citicorp Center in New
York Again.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:21.460
I picked this example on purpose because when
it was built it was actually built with one

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:29.340
of the most dangerous design flaws in history.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:34.219
To save money, the building wasn’t initially
constructed using the maximum number of reinforced

00:08:34.219 --> 00:08:38.009
joints and they weren’t made out of the
highest possible quality of steel--again,

00:08:38.009 --> 00:08:41.610
going back to the idea that better materials
have higher compression forces.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:46.480
It was discovered that if the wind blew on
the building at a diagonal, any hurricane-force

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:53.330
winds of 74 miles/hour hitting New York could
bring the entire building down, crashing onto

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:54.870
all the buildings in the area.

00:08:54.870 --> 00:09:00.940
Those sorts of winds hit New York about once
every fifty years, so obviously a massive

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:05.589
problem, but to save face, the architect worked
in secret with Citigroup to have all the bolts

00:09:05.589 --> 00:09:11.370
in the building reinforced at night with crews
only working after hours, and managed to fix

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:13.959
it right before hurricane season started.

00:09:13.959 --> 00:09:16.610
The story didn’t surface to the public for
over 20 years.

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:20.959
So yeah, this is not just a movie problem:
there have been some pretty high profile engineering

00:09:20.959 --> 00:09:25.670
oopsie-moments that have come close to costing
hundreds or thousands of lives around the

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:29.949
world, with the powers that be sometimes working
behind the scenes to cover them up.

00:09:29.949 --> 00:09:33.690
Of course, the stacks in Ready Player One
have no sophisticated computer systems counter-acting

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:37.720
the effects of wind like this -- all of their
sophisticated tech is dedicated to entertaining

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:38.720
the masses, silly!

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:42.970
But no matter how immersive that virtual reality
simulation is, it's going to be pretty hard

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:52.209
to ignore the fact that your Stack comes crashing
down with the first lake effect snowstorm

00:09:52.209 --> 00:09:56.110
of the year.

00:09:56.110 --> 00:10:02.410
But on top of just being not reinforced at
all, the Stacks are facing a double whammy

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:06.560
when it comes to wind problems because of
how narrow they are.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:10.660
Comparing them to a normal apartment building,
the "skinniest" apartment building in NYC

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:16.999
has an area of 3300 square feet, which is
more than five times the area of a 600 square

00:10:16.999 --> 00:10:18.180
foot trailer home.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:24.470
At 22 levels high, Wade's home is taller than
some skyscrapers, yet far thinner than even

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:25.779
the thinnest high rises.

00:10:25.779 --> 00:10:27.370
What does this mean?

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:31.000
Well, ever try to stack a single column of
LEGOs up on their own?

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:32.250
Notice anything in particular?

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:35.269
Maybe that they fall over really easily.

00:10:35.269 --> 00:10:39.209
When trying to figure out whether something
is easy to tip over--spoiler alert, the Stacks

00:10:39.209 --> 00:10:43.040
are SUPER easy to tip over, all you have to
do is find their center of gravity.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:44.040
[We need a graphic here: Wade's stack is 22
levels high, with each level consisting of

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:45.040
a 10-foot-tall trailer (plus 1 foot for supporting
materials), a total of 242 feet high.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:46.040
Most trailers in the stacks are single wide).]

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:49.089
Looking just at Wade's stack (22 trailer homes
high), if we assume a best-case scenario with

00:10:49.089 --> 00:10:54.230
uniform construction, that would put the center
of gravity between the 11th and 12th level,.

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:59.290
Running the numbers, if this stack tilts just
4.7 degrees, its center of gravity will no

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:03.399
longer be located over its base, leading the
entire thing to tumble over.

00:11:03.399 --> 00:11:09.570
A sway of 4.7 degrees could happen with gusts
of winds at 40 mph, maybe even less if you

00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:13.990
remember that the building also has no support
systems and no foundation.

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:18.899
Considering Ohio’s average windspeed sits
around 18 mph, the stacks are going to be

00:11:18.899 --> 00:11:21.259
in a perpetual state of leaning.

00:11:21.259 --> 00:11:25.241
Since 1950, more than 1,000 tornadoes have
touched down in Ohio.

00:11:25.241 --> 00:11:31.209
Ohio is no stranger to high speed winds sweeping
down the plains, so long story short, basically

00:11:31.209 --> 00:11:35.600
anyone breathing on this thing too hard means
it’s game over for Wade.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:39.449
But ok, the Stacks are violating some of the
basic principles of civil engineering, and

00:11:39.449 --> 00:11:40.960
they're absurdly unsafe.

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:41.960
“So what?”

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:44.749
I hear you saying: Of course they're unsafe,
this is a dystopia!

00:11:44.749 --> 00:11:49.589
They don't exactly have safety in mind when
they made these stacks! Wade even describes

00:11:49.589 --> 00:11:54.999
the "domino"-like effect when a stack collapses
and in the trailer we see a stack completely

00:11:54.999 --> 00:11:58.420
obliterated by what seems to be a relatively
small explosion.

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:03.220
These buildings aren’t designed to be strong;
they’re designed to be cheap.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:13.769
Buuuut that’s the 
biggest problem, you see, they’re NOT cheap.

00:12:13.769 --> 00:12:18.759
And this is where we encounter the biggest
problem with the stacks: they're designed

00:12:18.759 --> 00:12:24.850
for one thing, to cut costs on dense urban
housing -- and they even suck at that!

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:31.230
In fact, they’re way more expensive to produce
than good old fashioned concrete housing.

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:35.290
Concrete is seriously cheap: it’s literally
made of sand, something that it’s safe to

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:38.540
say we will not be running out of in the next
25 years.

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:41.839
Concrete housing is currently the primary
component in urban construction where there’s

00:12:41.839 --> 00:12:46.500
a massive need for more urban homes: The national
average cost of building an apartment building

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:54.000
in the US is currently about $75 per square
foot or about $45,000 for 600 square feet,

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.220
the same size as our single-wide trailer.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.870
But that’s using mid or even higher quality
materials.

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:04.010
What we’re looking for in a Ready Player
One scenario are known as Class 6 building

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:09.000
materials in the US, which consist of reinforced
concrete and wood framing, but still has all

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:11.689
the major appliances and safety features.

00:13:11.689 --> 00:13:20.829
These buildings are as low as $57.39 per square
foot, or $34,434 for our 600 square foot home.

00:13:20.829 --> 00:13:27.339
By contrast, a new single-wide mobile home
costs costs about $40,000 and that jumps up

00:13:27.339 --> 00:13:30.170
to about $75,000 if you’re in a double-wide.

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:34.620
And that’s just the trailer, that’s not
the cost of getting it all the way on top

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:38.810
of the nearest Stack, building extra scaffolding
around it, or extending wires and cables higher

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:41.509
into the air so residents can get their dose
of OASIS on the daily.

00:13:41.509 --> 00:13:45.509
And we’re not even MENTIONING the cpst of
rebuilding these thigs when catastrophic failures

00:13:45.509 --> 00:13:50.649
occur -- remember, Wade sopecifically mentions
multitple instances of domino collapses.

00:13:50.649 --> 00:13:54.860
And if you’re still on the fence about it,
remember that building codes and inspections

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:58.639
are all factored into the current cost of
apartment buildings in the US, so if they’re

00:13:58.639 --> 00:14:02.529
willing to just skip that step in Ready Player
One, which it seems like they have no problem

00:14:02.529 --> 00:14:11.100
doing, you can look at seriously slashing
prices on the housing in this movie...oh and

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:13.319
saving like hundreds of lives in the process.

00:14:13.319 --> 00:14:15.360
But whatever, not like that matters or anything.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:18.930
So when you head out to see Ready Player One,
appreciate all the cool technology, dream

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:24.520
of futuristic MMORPGs, but shake your head
knowing that the most unbelievable, and the

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:30.529
most unbelievably deadly part of this fiction
is literally the house it takes place in.

00:14:30.529 --> 00:14:35.589
But hey, that's just a theory -- a FILM theory.

00:14:35.589 --> 00:14:37.920
Thanks for watching!

